sections.s2_technical:
Start with the cached pool internals. `Executors.newCachedThreadPool()` is `new ThreadPoolExecutor(0, Integer.MAX_VALUE, 60L, SECONDS, new SynchronousQueue<>())`. A `SynchronousQueue` has no capacity: an `offer` succeeds only if a thread is already waiting to take. So when a task is submitted, the executor first tries to hand it to an idle thread; if none is waiting, because the maximum is effectively unlimited, it immediately creates a new thread. There is no queue to absorb the backlog and no ceiling to stop growth.

This design is perfect for short tasks because a thread finishes quickly, becomes idle, and is reused by the next task, so the pool stays small. But with long tasks the reuse never happens. For example, if the bank submits 10,000 slow fraud-check tasks that each take 30 seconds, the pool will try to create up to 10,000 threads because none finish in time to be reused. At roughly one megabyte of stack per thread that is about 10 gigabytes of stack memory, plus the operating system often caps threads per process, so you hit `OutOfMemoryError: unable to create new native thread` and the JVM dies.

```java
ExecutorService dangerous = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
    dangerous.submit(() -> {
        Thread.sleep(30_000);          // long task → thread never reused
        return runFraudCheck("SAV2001");
    });
}
// → thousands of threads created → OutOfMemoryError
```

The safe alternative is a bounded `ThreadPoolExecutor`: a fixed or capped maximum pool size, a bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue`, and a `RejectedExecutionHandler` such as `CallerRunsPolicy`. This caps both threads and queued work, so under overload the submitting thread slows down (back-pressure) instead of the heap filling up.

```java
ThreadPoolExecutor safe = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        32, 32, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000),
        new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());   // back-pressure, not explosion
```

Now the ForkJoin common pool. The common pool is a single shared `ForkJoinPool` used by parallel streams and `CompletableFuture` default executors, sized to `availableProcessors() - 1`. Its contract assumes tasks are CPU-bound and rarely block. To keep the promised parallelism when a task does block, the framework offers `ManagedBlocker`. When you block through a managed blocker, the pool notices a worker is parked and spawns a compensating thread so the remaining work still has the intended number of active threads.

The explosion happens when many tasks block at the same time. Each blocking worker triggers a compensating thread, and if that compensating thread also blocks, another is spawned, and so on. In a parallel stream doing blocking HTTP calls, you can end up with hundreds of threads far above the core count, all contending, context switching, and consuming memory. Even ordinary blocking (a plain `Thread.sleep` or JDBC call without a managed blocker) is harmful in a different way: it does not spawn compensation, so the fixed set of common-pool workers all get stuck, and every other part of the application that relies on the common pool (any parallel stream anywhere) stalls behind them.

```java
// DANGEROUS: blocking HTTP inside a parallel stream uses the COMMON pool
accountIds.parallelStream()
          .map(id -> httpClient.get("/balance/" + id))   // blocks each worker
          .collect(Collectors.toList());
// → common pool workers stall; CompletableFutures elsewhere also stall
```

The safe alternatives for blocking work are two. First, never run blocking IO on the common pool: give `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync` an explicit dedicated executor sized for IO, and avoid parallel streams for blocking operations. Second, if you must block inside a fork/join context, do it through `ForkJoinPool.managedBlock(ManagedBlocker)` so the pool can compensate in a controlled way — but a dedicated bounded pool is almost always the better design. A senior nuance is to size the common pool at startup with `-Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism=N` only as a tuning aid, never as a substitute for keeping blocking work off it.

